Within the same chat that produced the Bresenham line algorithm, I also worked with AI to generate these methods about orientation of vectors. Since I had accidentally deleted that
session with AI I no longer have the transcript I used to work out these methods either. But, essentially I needed a way to calculate the orientation of one line segment to another, 
that is to determine whether or not the angle between them was positive or negative. This would allow me to determine which side of a line segment a point lied on 
and ultimately I could use this, in combination with some other methods to determine if a line that im drawing intersects with any class box boundaries. 
The standard way to do this I found out was to calculate the signed area of the parallelogram spanned by two line segments sharing a common point, this amounts to calculating their determinant.
The sign of area is what indicates the position of the point relative to the line segment. In addition if the area is equal to 0, then the segments would be collinear.
The following method was used for this calculation.

public int orientation(Point2D a, Point2D b, Point2D c){
        double val = (b.getX() - a.getX()) * (c.getY() - a.getY()) - (b.getY() - a.getY())*(c.getX() - a.getX());
        double epsilon = 1e-9;
        if(Math.abs(val) < epsilon) return 0;
        return (val > 0) ? 1 : 2;

    }

With this in hand, it didn't require much more prompting to determine a way to know whether or not two line segments intersect. A simple geometry theorem that states if the end points of a line segment A
lie on opposite sides of a line segment B and similarly if the two endpoints of the line segment B lie on opposite sides of the line segment A, then the two segments intersect. If this fails to be true
, then we must check for collinearity of the segments, i.e. the area of the signed parallelogram = 0, and then we need to determine whether or not one or both of the endpoints of a line segment A lie along
the segment B. If so, we know the two segments intersect. If all of these tests fail, we could safely conclude that the two segments don't intersect. Within the context of the A* algorithm, we would know
there isn't a class box obstacle blocking the path we were trying to build.

private boolean segmentsIntersect(Point2D p1, Point2D q1, Point2D p2, Point2D q2){
        int o1 = orientation(p1, q1, p2);
        int o2 = orientation(p1, q1, q2);
        int o3 = orientation(p2, q2, p1);
        int o4 = orientation(p2, q2, q1);
        
        if(o1 != o2 && o3 != o4){
            return true;
        }
        
        if(o1 == 0 && onSegment(p1, q1, p2))
            return true;
        if(o2 == 0 && onSegment(p1, q1, q2))
            return true;
        if(o3 == 0 && onSegment(p2, q2, p1))
            return true;
        if(o4 == 0 && onSegment(p2, q2, q1))
            return true;
        return false;
    }

public boolean onSegment(Point2D a, Point2D b, Point2D c){
        return c.getX() <= Math.max(a.getX(), b.getX()) &&
                c.getX() >= Math.min(a.getX(), b.getX()) &&
                c.getY() <= Math.max(a.getY(), b.getY()) &&
                c.getY() >= Math.min(a.getY(), b.getY());
    }

public boolean segmentIntersectsRectangle(Point2D a, Point2D b, Rectangle2D rect){
        
        Point2D topLeft = new Point2D(rect.getMinX(), rect.getMinY());
        Point2D topRight = new Point2D(rect.getMaxX(), rect.getMinY());
        Point2D bottomLeft = new Point2D(rect.getMinX(), rect.getMaxY());
        Point2D bottomRight = new Point2D(rect.getMaxX(), rect.getMaxY());
        if(rect.contains(a) || rect.contains(b)){
            return true;
        }
        //Next check whether ab intersects any of the four edges of the rectangle.
        if(segmentsIntersect(a, b, topLeft, topRight)) {
            return true;
        }
        if(segmentsIntersect(a, b, topRight, bottomRight)){
            return true;
        }
        if(segmentsIntersect(a, b, bottomRight, bottomLeft)){
            return true;
        }
        if(segmentsIntersect(a, b, bottomLeft, topLeft)){
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }

}


